# modules/image/checks-advisories.rules - the container-image module's own
# per-owner registry (rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9's `checks-<name>.rules` row,
# docs/FOUNDATION.md tension 29) for the advisory-database coverage check
# IMG-03 adds.
#
# ONE FILE PER OWNING DRIVER, per every other module's own precedent
# (modules/dast/passive/'s six checks-<name>.rules files, modules/network/'s
# five) - this ticket's OWN file rather than an append to a shared one, per
# the "one registry per owner" binding rule and the cloud-wave incident it cites
# (`c9f0e80`): a shared modules/image/checks.rules would be exactly the
# add/add conflict class every later IMG-0x ticket would then collide on.
#
# `script: run.sh` because IMAGE-COV-NO_ADVISORY_DB-01 is emitted by the
# module's own driver (modules/image/run.sh, via
# modules/image/engine.sh's image_report_no_advisory_db), not by a
# distro-specific phase script - there is no modules/image/distro/apk.sh on
# disk yet (IMG-04), and this check fires whether or not one ever does for
# THIS image's distro.
#
# `tags: passive` - the closest fit among rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9.1.3's
# fixed vocabulary (static/passive/safe-active/active/config-read/posture):
# this check reads a local file (data/advisories.db) and a local os-release
# byte string already extracted from the image, makes no network call, and
# mutates nothing - modules/network/'s own checks-banner.rules and
# checks-transport.rules apply the identical tag to their own read-only,
# no-mutation checks. `quick` because the whole check costs nothing beyond a
# single db_lookup_exact prefix test - the same "cheapest real coverage a
# run can buy" reasoning modules/dast/passive/checks-tls.rules's own header
# gives its own `quick` tag.

id: IMAGE-COV-NO_ADVISORY_DB-01
title: Container image scanning did NOT run - no advisory database rows for this image's distro release
script: run.sh
severity: info
confidence: high
cwe: none
owasp: none
tags: passive
tags: quick
coverage-scope: image-id
remediation: Populate data/advisories.db for this image's distro/release
  ecosystem (for example Alpine:v3.18) with 'tools/vendor-engines.sh
  advisories alpine' (or the matching distro importer, once one exists) on a
  networked box, then re-run. Until then this run says NOTHING about this
  image's installed packages - absence of findings here is absence of
  evidence, never evidence of absence.
